course: dictionary
lesson: 10075
title: One word, many jobs: that
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word that is small, but it has many jobs. Sometimes it points at something: «that box over there» means one particular box, the one farther away.
explain: Sometimes that joins two parts of a sentence, like «I know that 2 + 2 is 4». Here it links what you know to the rest of the sentence.
explain: Sometimes that describes a thing and means which or who: «the cake that Mum made». It also can mean so or very: «It was not that hard». To find its job, read the words after it and ask: is this pointing, joining, or describing?
fact: That can be a pronoun, a determiner, a conjunction or an adverb, depending on the job it does.
fact: «That» points to a thing named before or farther away, like «that box over there».
fact: «That» can join a sentence to what someone says or thinks, like «I know that 2 + 2 is 4».
fact: «That» can mean which or who in a description: «the cake that Mum made».
fact: «That» can mean so or very, as in «It was not that hard», and phrases like «that's right» and «after that» are common.
exercise: In «The cake that Mum made was gone.» what job does that do? | *It tells you which cake, like which or who | It points to a thing over there | It means very, as in very big | It means that the cake is finished
exercise: «I know that 2 + 2 is 4.» What job does that do here? | It describes a noun | *It joins the sentence to what you know | It points to a box | It means so or very
exercise: «It was not that hard.» What does that mean here? | Which | Over there | Finished | *Very, or so
test: «I like that box over there.» What does that point to? | The box in your hand | Nothing at all | Your favourite cake | *The box you can see, farther away
test: Mum says, «That's it, we're done.» What does that's it mean? | The cake is gone | *The work is finished | It is a rude word | We are starting now
exam: In «The black box helps investigators find out why, so that it never happens again», what job does that do in so that? | It means very, as in very tired | It describes a noun, like which | *It joins the reason to the result | It points to a black box over there
